---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 13: Cut &amp; Evaluate Your Practice Build</h1>
        
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 13</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Cut Your Practice Build</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Real Material Today</h2>
              <p>
                Four weeks of design, materials, safety, and CAM &mdash; all
                of it, running on the actual machine.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Before Anything: Your Checklist</h2>
              <p>
                Walk your Session 10 pre-cut checklist one more time,
                against this exact job, before touching the machine.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Every Session 4 Protocol Still Applies</h2>
              <p>
                Certified, supervised, one job at a time &mdash; nothing
                about today changes those rules.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Sequence</h2>
              <ol>
                <li class="fragment">Confirm material is secured</li>
                <li class="fragment">Verify zero/home</li>
                <li class="fragment">Run the job, supervised, start to finish</li>
                <li class="fragment">Remove tabs by hand once cutting is done</li>
              </ol>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>If Something Goes Wrong</h2>
              <p>
                Stop the job. Diagnose using Session 10's symptom list
                before assuming anything.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>A Failed First Attempt Isn't a Failed Session</h2>
              <p>
                Diagnosing a real problem, correctly, is worth as much as a
                clean first cut &mdash; same philosophy as Unit 1's Mismatch
                Activity.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Watch the whole cut.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Never leave a running job unattended &mdash; Session 4's
                rule, still true, no exceptions today.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Keep the part, even if it's imperfect.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Next session is about evaluating what actually happened
                &mdash; you need the real result, not just a memory of it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Then: Bring Back the Calipers</h2>
              <p>
                While other teams are on the machine, you are not waiting
                &mdash; you are evaluating. Same tool as Unit 1 Session 6,
                same discipline, real stakes this time.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Measure what actually matters: outer dimensions, pocket
                depth, hole location.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Mismatch Isn't a Failed Session</h2>
              <p>
                A part that came out 2&nbsp;mm undersized with a correct
                diagnosis is worth more than a part that happened to come out
                right for reasons nobody can name.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Four Weeks, One Thread</h2>
              <p>
                Client communication &rarr; materials &rarr; CAD &rarr; CAM
                &rarr; safety. Every one of them shows up in the part in your
                hand.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Up</h2>
              <p>The real brief arrives.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Same five skills, a real client, and a cabinet that has to
                work.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Final Checklist Pass</h3>
          <p>Walk your Session 10 pre-cut checklist one more time against your actual Session 12 job file.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Load, Secure, Zero</h3>
          <p>
            Load your stock, confirm it's fully secured, and verify your
            zero point &mdash; per Session 4's protocols.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Run the Job, Supervised</h3>
          <p>
            Run the job start to finish. Stay with the machine the entire
            time.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Remove Tabs and Inspect</h3>
          <p>
            Once the job finishes, remove any tabs by hand and take a first
            look at your part. Check for tear-out, tab marks not fully
            cleaned up, or any surface roughness, and note anything you find.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 5: Measure Your Part</h3>
          <p>
            Calipers, real readings, not estimates. Do this at your bench
            while other teams are on the machine.
          </p>

          <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Designed</th><th>Measured</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Outer length</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Outer width</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Pocket depth</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Hole location</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 6: Diagnose Any Mismatch</h3>
          <p>
            For any dimension that differs from your design by more than a
            small amount, use Sessions 7&ndash;10's concepts to explain why
            &mdash; offset, step-down, material behavior, or something else.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 7: Unit Recap</h3>
          <p>
            List the five main skills from this 4-week arc: client
            communication, materials (subtractive and additive), CAD, CAM,
            and safety certification. For each, write one sentence on how it
            showed up in your practice build specifically.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you leave the machine, confirm:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>You stayed with the job for its entire run</li>
            <li>You have the physical part in hand, whatever its condition</li>
            <li>If anything went wrong, you have a first-guess diagnosis written down, not just "it didn't work"</li>
            <li>Real measured dimensions in your table, not estimates</li>
            <li>A specific diagnosis for any mismatch &mdash; not "it just came out wrong"</li>
            <li>All five Manufacturing Foundations skills named in your recap</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Did anything go wrong during the cut? If so, give your first-guess diagnosis using Session 10's symptom list. If nothing went wrong, name the symptom you were most watching for.</li>
            <li>Of the five Manufacturing Foundations skills, which one do you feel least confident applying to a real, higher-stakes project starting next week?</li>
            <li>This is the last session before a real client brief arrives. What's one thing about your own process you want to do differently once the stakes are real?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your completed checklist pass, your measurement table, your mismatch diagnosis (if any), your unit recap, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Manufacturing Foundations Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
